A conversion to a newtype whose base type is inferred, such as `newtype Y0 = six | Yes(six)` where `Yes` takes a `NewSix`, now checks the constraints of that base type. Before, it checked only the newtype's own constraint, while every value of the newtype is assumed to satisfy its base's constraints too, so `3 as Y0` was accepted although `NewSix` requires `6 <= x`, and a program could prove `false`. The same applies to a literal of such a newtype and to conversions from `int`, `real` and bitvector values. The hidden option `--legacy-inferred-newtype-base-conversions` restores the old, unsound check, for comparison and migration only.
